Long time reader, first time poster.

This was my second ascension, the first one was a neutral human wizard
on my home machine quite some time ago. Now I'm playing random
characters on NAO, which took some getting used to. But it really
helped my game, I learned a lot the past few months.

This orcish barbarian had a pretty easy time in the early game. I found
a +5 pair of iron shoes early in the mines and wore them for the rest
of the game. Minetown was a bones file, which got me a couple of
enchant armor scrolls, some scrolls of genocide and a pre-blessed
luckstone, so I didn't go any deeper into the mines. Without MR
polytraps really scare me.

Working my way down in the main dungeon, I am greeted by Yeenoghu when
I step into the rogue level. Checked the spoiler, looked at my HP/AC
and decided to just whack him to death. Unfortunately HE was standing
on the up stairs, not me. So he flees straightaway. I followed him up,
but he and an elf teamed up on me, so it wasn't looking very good. I
dug down and didn't go up for quite some time. Fortunately I had a BoH
full of useful stuff on me. Found an altar and uncursed the two BoH
from the double bones file in the rogue level. Swimming in useful kit,
including a +3 silver dragon scale mail,
I took my time to ID everything before taking on anything major.

I decided to wear a ring of free action, after a recent bad experience
with the Minetown guard (don't walk around Minetown wielding
Stormbringer...) This turns out to be a very good call: two turns after
I get back to the level with Yeenoghu, a hobbit trows a potion of
paralysis on my head. Pretty buff and feeling invincible, I went on my
quest. It was pretty easy, until Thoth Amon starts summoning a whole
bunch of strong creatures. Fortunately, I could just beat him to death
and run with my Bell and the Heart.

Medusa had a peaceful gremlin, which started multiplying beyond control
before I could kill him. A ring of conflict kept me from having to kill
them all (which woudn't have made me very popular with my god). In the
mazes between Medusa and the Castle, I come across a curved wand.
That's odd, I thought I had identified all of them.... except wishing!
Seven extra wishes, that always makes the game easier.

>From then on it was pretty uneventful for a long time. Before Gehennom
I switched from Cleaver to dual-wielding Magicbane and a silver saber.
Nothing could really do me much damage, I had wished up some nice armor
and markers for enchantment and fixing. I found two more bags of
holding (five in total, I believe), another wand of wishing (good for 6
wishes) and two magic lamps (one of which was pre-blessed).

The only scare was early on in my run with the Amulet, when Rodney
came, took the Amulet and double troubled me. He and his clone summon a
horde of strong creatures and just sit down the corridor (at the
up-stair) laughing at me. That proved to be their undoing. One of the
summoned monsters is a black dragon. It tries to disintegrate me, the
beam misses and disintegrates one Rodney. Few turns later, the second
one suffers the same faith. I thanked the dragon by not killing it.

The planes, except Astral, were pretty straightforward. Astral was
nasty. As you might be able to tell from the dumplog below, I had to
visit all the altars before finding my own. The riders didn't bother me
too much, I lost 20 max HP (out of 412) to Death, and emptied a WoD on
the others. It's the priests and angels that really pack a punch up
there. I got to as low as 250 HP at some point; my total actually
turned yellow, it hadn't done that in ages;-). The moral of the story
is, come prepared, pay attention and you'll be okay.

-- Arjen

